<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">company</ansi>

Your company is you, the leader, plus up to 4 companions who fight and
travel beside you. They stand with you in formation
(<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>), share your chemistry in battle
(<ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>), and need food and rest just like
you do (<ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Experience</ansi>

Companions grow. When a kill earns you experience, every companion who is
alive and standing in the room where the enemy fell earns the same amount,
in full: nothing is split, so a bigger company never shrinks your share.
A companion who is away, fallen, or still waiting to rejoin earns
nothing. Enough experience is a new level, and you're told; the
companion keeps the health and mana it had
(<ansi fg="command">help readiness</ansi>). Companions have no level cap, and no points for you to
spend: their archetype deals each earned stat point, and
<ansi fg="command">company growth</ansi> sets a focus (<ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>).
A contract quest also pays every companion with you when you turn it in
(<ansi fg="command">help contracts</ansi>). <ansi fg="command">experience</ansi> lists each companion's level and
progress below your own (<ansi fg="command">help experience</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Readiness</ansi>

Companions keep their health, mana, and wounds when you log out, across a
restart, and through a crash (as of the last save): nothing refills them
for free. Out of a fight they recover as you do while you're logged in,
and a night at an inn restores everyone (<ansi fg="command">help readiness</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Finding companions</ansi>

Settlements have recruiters: a person or a posted notice offering
candidates for hire. <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> in a recruiter's room shows the
notice and the candidates you can still take, with their price: "free"
for a once-only offer, or "won't join you" for one your company would
refuse (see Alignment, below). Anyone already in your company, and a free
offer you've already taken, isn't listed; a full company is noted.
<ansi fg="command">look post</ansi> (or the notice's name) reads the notice again.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your own recruits</ansi>

Settlement recruiters (the Waymark Inn's hiring slate, the Trappers'
Post) also post recruits of your own: 3 at a time as the world ships,
listed after the regulars. Each is a new face with their own name, archetype, level (within
one of yours), alignment, price, and a line about them. Your list is yours
alone: another player at the same notice sees different people, and what
you hire never changes their list, or theirs yours.

The list changes over time. Each recruit waits a while (half a game day
to two game days, as the world ships), then moves on and someone new takes their place, one
face at a time; time you spend away counts. Hire one and their place
stays empty for a little while before someone new turns up. One your
company would refuse is marked "won't join you", the same as any other
candidate. At the recruiter, type a recruit's first name to hire, look
at, or inspect them (<ansi fg="command">company recruit hild</ansi>). Once hired, they keep their
own name in your company, even if two of them are the same archetype.

  <ansi fg="command">look [candidate]</ansi>
  Read a candidate's description on the notice.

  <ansi fg="command">company recruit</ansi>
  List who is hiring here, with their archetype, level, alignment, gear,
  and price.

  <ansi fg="command">company recruit [candidate]</ansi>
  Take one on. Most candidates cost gold; some are offered free, but only
  once ever, per account. If your company won't have them (see
  Alignment, below), they say so instead of joining.

  <ansi fg="command">company inspect [candidate]</ansi>
  Check a candidate's alignment against your company's before you pay:
  whether they would join, and whether they would join uneasily.

<ansi fg="command">company summon [name]</ansi> enlists a companion directly,
without a recruiter, but only the few the world allows (a training dummy,
as the world ships).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Moving together</ansi>

Your companions follow you from room to room. You aren't told each one
arriving; the rooms you leave and enter hear one line for the whole
company ("Dain leads their company towards the east exit.", "Dain arrives
from the west, their company behind."). Any other move (a companion on
its own, or following you after a flight) still shows its own line.
<ansi fg="command">look</ansi> lists your companions by name; in a party of
players, <ansi fg="command">party</ansi> lists them as Company.

Every other way you're moved (a passage, a portal, a journey's arrival,
waking at a church) takes the companions standing with you. One who
isn't with you is separated and finds its way back in about a minute
(<ansi fg="command">help separation</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your roster</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi>
  Every companion's number, name, level, archetype, alignment, loyalty,
  and whether they're present, awaiting restoration, separated (and how
  long until they're back), fallen, or lost for good.

  <ansi fg="command">company gear [member]</ansi>
  What a companion is wearing; unworn items are shared cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">company inventory</ansi>
  Everything the whole company carries, its horses, and the cargo
  (<ansi fg="command">help company inventory</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">company eat</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">company drink</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">company meal</ansi>
  Feed and water everyone at once (<ansi fg="command">help company meal</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">company dismiss [member]</ansi>
  Let a companion go. <ansi fg="command">company dismiss all</ansi> lets your
  whole company go.

A fallen companion stays on your roster, waiting to be raised at a church
or a village shaman (<ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi>). Wait too long
and they're lost for good; <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi> shows
anyone lost.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Archetype</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company archetype [member] [archetype]</ansi>
  Give a companion without one their archetype. This is permanent, the
  same as your own choice (<ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>). It sets
  what they can be trained to do and which utility skills they can use
  for the company (<ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">company growth [member] [stat]</ansi>
  Favour one stat as their level's points are dealt, or
  <ansi fg="command">balanced</ansi> to clear it; <ansi fg="command">company growth</ansi> alone
  shows everyone's (<ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Tactics</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi>
  Whom your whole company goes for in a battle (the focus) and when your
  healers heal (the healing threshold). The focus is the one order you can
  change in the middle of a battle. See <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Alignment and chemistry</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company alignment</ansi>
  Your company's alignment: yours, each companion's, and the average.
  See <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi> for what it means and how it
  drifts.

  <ansi fg="command">company chemistry</ansi>
  How well your company fights together after time served side by side.
  See <ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Specialists</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company specialists</ansi>
  Who in your company reads trails, spots secret exits, haggles,
  forecasts the weather, and more, outside a fight. Recruit for the
  skills an expedition needs. See <ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>.

In the web client, the Company tab shows the same: its Status, Inventory,
and Camp (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Member orders</ansi>

Members fight automatically. Safe <ansi fg="command">ask [member]</ansi> orders
manage gear and provisions outside battles; conversation and observation
remain available in battle. Old queued orders recheck ownership and presence.
See <ansi fg="command">help ask</ansi> for the compatibility commands.

Group healing reaches your present company without a player party. A chant
rechecks life, presence and ownership, and healing stops at wound limits
(<ansi fg="command">help friendly-effects</ansi>).

Leaving a battle: <ansi fg="command">retreat [exit]</ansi> (or <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>) prepares for one round,
then attempts to move the company together. See <ansi fg="command">help retreat</ansi>.

For player alliances and opt-in following/support, see <ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>.
See also: <ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help contracts</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help separation</ansi>.

Shared cargo and deliberate equipment assignment: <ansi fg="command">help equipment</ansi>.
Pooled gold: <ansi fg="command">help treasury</ansi>. Battle spoils: <ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>.

Browser Company Status shows owned members' active effects and wounds,
with duration and mechanical meaning, apart from persistent bonuses.
Away, fallen and unavailable conditions are labelled explicitly.
See <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>.
